    "summary": "Puntland's leader Said Abdullahi Deni has declared that the regional state will govern itself independently in its eastern districts, a move amid ongoing tensions with Somalia's central government over constitutional reforms.",
    "content": "During a visit to remote eastern areas of Puntland on Tuesday, President Said Abdullahi Deni announced a major shift in governance: the region's eastern territories will now be administered autonomously, akin to a sovereign state. The declaration comes after months of disagreements between Puntland and the federal government of Somalia, primarily over proposed constitutional changes that Puntland officials argue undermine their autonomy.\n\nDeni, who has been a vocal critic of the federal government's recent policies, stated that the decision was necessary to protect the interests of Puntland’s residents, especially those in the eastern regions that border Somalia's semi-autonomous areas. He emphasized that this move is not about secession but about ensuring effective service delivery and security in the absence of clear federal cooperation.\n\nAnalysts note that this is not the first time Puntland has flexed its political muscle against Mogadishu. In 1998, Puntland declared itself an autonomous state, and it has since called for a federal system that grants more powers to regional governments. The announcement follows recent federal moves to centralize authority, which Puntland officials see as a violation of the federal constitution.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant because Puntland is home to a large portion of the population with family ties abroad. Diaspora remittances and investments are vital to Puntland's economy, and any shift in governance could affect how these funds are managed. Additionally, diaspora members often play key roles in political and economic discussions, making stability in Puntland crucial for their engagement.\n\nObservers will watch closely to see how the federal government responds to this declaration. The move could deepen the rift between Mogadishu and regional states, potentially impacting the upcoming elections and the broader stability of Somalia. For now, Deni’s announcement signals a firm stance, but the practical implications will unfold in the coming weeks.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/madaxweyne-deni-oo-ku-dhawaaqay-in-puntland-isu-maamulayso-sidii-dowlad-madax-banaan/425466/",
